From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:52:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Live: face-shaped paste-back mask from a face parser (on by
 default)

The swap was blended through a fixed feathered oval. A BiSeNet face parser
(FaceFusion's bisenet_resnet_18, CelebAMask-HQ labels) now finds the real
face in the aligned crop - skin, brows, eyes, nose, mouth, lips - and the
swap is pasted through that mask (combined with HyperSwap's own mask and a
template that only fades the crop edges), so it follows the jaw and
hairline and leaves hair, ears, hands and hats alone.

The parser is trimmed to one output (aux heads pruned, softmax + 1x1 conv
summing the face classes) and runs on a background thread on the newest
crop; frames paste with the newest mask, which lives in aligned space.
It parses synchronously on the first frame, for still images, when the
mask is stale and when the alignment jumps (another face, a cut).
~45 ms per face on Apple Silicon (GPU), so it costs frame rate there; it
can be switched off in the Quality card ("Face-shaped mask").

Applio-Commit: 47890c1c108c635d1ec90b92b966d26af2203415
